  "Analysis": [
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:typeOfAnalysis",
      "object": "Correlation"
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Wearing a uniform that emphasizes body concerns predicted increases in weight/shape concern during the 4-month observation period. Wearing a uniform that emphasizes body concerns did not predict increases in restrained eating behaviors. Perceived pressure for thinness from coaches predicted an increase in both weight/shape concern and restrained eating behaviors at time 2. Teammate pressure for thinness was solely predictive of restrained eating behaviors at time 2. Coach pressure for thinness was the only athlete-specific risk factor that was predictive of increases in both weight/shape concerns and restrained eating behaviors."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The study time frame was relatively short, which may have limited the ability to detect change in weight/shape concern and restrained eating behaviors. Our sample had substantial attrition, with only about 54% of participants who completed time 1 completed the follow-up survey. The director of athletics and associated staff were present for the data collection ; some athletes may have been concerned that the athletics department would have access to their data. There was a limited number of club athlete participants in the final sample, level of sport competitive could not be considered. There may be differences in competition level between club sport versus division I athletes. There are a number of measurement considerations."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future would also should assess team culture and/or group cohesion, as these may buffer against the negative impact of perceived pressure to be thin from teammates and/or coaches, as teammates can be protective against, or heighten the risk for, disordered eating. \nFor athletic departments to offer training to coaches that includes learning indicators of eating disorder relapse."
    },
